#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51261d45-18aa-565e-bd5e-0de7772a7f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}+\\frac{5}{(x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354316} {:answer/id #uuid "2de8007c-efb4-50d2-9fac-a556222eba6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354317} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cfb5b7-a323-5137-8dd9-6980395cee4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354318} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc02512-1729-5caf-ba08-55e1876ec02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-1}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}-\\frac{4}{(x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354319} {:answer/id #uuid "9083c75f-a0c5-5f36-b62c-9fbf205ed4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x-2}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}-\\frac{2}{(x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9cfb5b7-a323-5137-8dd9-6980395cee4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354318} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b91643-5355-5918-9758-59dee221b7f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed503c06-b57c-5cdc-af73-5c39124e5788" :question/math-academy-id "q-102568" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{4{x}^{2}+10x+6}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}+10x+6}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)(x+3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}+\\frac{C}{(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+3x+4$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $\\frac{4{x}^{2}+10x+6}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)(x+3)}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+10x+6=(Ax+B)(x+3)+C({x}^{2}+3x+4).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-3)^{2}+10(-3)+6 & =(A(-3)+B)(-3+3)+C((-3)^{2}+3(-3)+4) \\\\ 36-30+6 & =4C \\\\ 12 & =4C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+10(0)+6 & =(A(0)+B)(0+3)+C({0}^{2}+3(0)+4) \\\\ 6 & =3B+4C \\\\ 6 & =3B+4(3) \\\\ 3B & =6-12 \\\\ B & =\\frac{-6}{3} \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{2}+10(1)+6 & =(A(1)+B)(1+3)+C({1}^{2}+3(1)+4) \\\\ 20 & =4A+4B+8C \\\\ 20 & =4A+4(-2)+8(3) \\\\ 4A & =20+8-24 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1,B=-2,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+10x+6}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)(x+3)}=\\frac{x-2}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eab7f8c6-e90b-59f9-9582-572d13da153b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}+\\frac{5}{(x-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354323} {:answer/id #uuid "33c3ab64-1998-5867-bfd1-a50d2fcb9d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}+\\frac{3}{(x-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354324} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd6d45f-9ded-520b-b23e-252f9516a4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}-\\frac{3}{(x-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354325} {:answer/id #uuid "7304a0de-2e97-5fac-b324-7bf43228622b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}-\\frac{4}{(x-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354326} {:answer/id #uuid "8f477b23-cc79-59c6-a024-c5289aa51a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}+\\frac{5}{(x-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eab7f8c6-e90b-59f9-9582-572d13da153b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}+\\frac{5}{(x-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354323} :answer-field/id #uuid "350000aa-cc96-585b-90c7-198678f19cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d6aaf9d-b896-5612-9d3e-9a2c1d285c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-102574" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-29x+34}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)(x-3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}-29x+34}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)(x-3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}-4x+5$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}-4x+5)(x-3)$ and get\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-29x+34=(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}-4x+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(3)^{2}-29(3)+34 & =(A(3)+B)(3-3)+C({3}^{2}-4(3)+5) \\\\ 63-87+34 & =2C \\\\ C & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-29(0)+34 & =(A(0)+B)(0-3)+C({0}^{2}-4(0)+5) \\\\ 34 & =-3B+5C \\\\ 34 & =-3B+5(5) \\\\ 3B & =25-34 \\\\ B & =\\frac{-9}{3} \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)^{2}-29(1)+34 & =(A(1)+B)(1-3)+C({1}^{2}-4(1)+5) \\\\ 12 & =-2A-2B+2C \\\\ 12 & =-2A-2(-3)+2(5) \\\\ 2A & =-12+6+10 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=5.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-29x+34}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)(x-3)}=\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}+\\frac{5}{(x-3)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dadd1bc-6e62-5df1-aa96-345cfbf16446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{5}{(x-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266465} {:answer/id #uuid "344b7faf-5dc5-5997-9d98-b47327a2de03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{3}{(x-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266466} {:answer/id #uuid "988485f1-78d4-5f96-933b-e0321a237844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266467} {:answer/id #uuid "de278ca4-78da-5edb-809f-bb893cb8d692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{4}{(x-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266468} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc33aed-910f-542d-89a5-e3b9b93d9eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bc33aed-910f-542d-89a5-e3b9b93d9eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266469} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bbd6bfd-645d-5d9f-a3f7-cc2dca25c75a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79d6f845-0357-59f6-a764-58bacb7343f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-11000" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-3x+1=(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)^{2}-3(2)+1 & =(A(2)+B)(2-2)+C({2}^{2}+3) \\\\ 12-6+1 & =7C \\\\ C & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}-3(0)+1 & =(A(0)+B)(0-2)+C({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ 1 & =-2B+3C \\\\ 1 & =-2B+3(1) \\\\ 2B & =2 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3(1)+1 & =(A(1)+B)(1-2)+C({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ 1 & =-A-B+4C \\\\ 1 & =-A-1+4(1) \\\\ A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=2,$ $B=1$ and $C=1.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)}=\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "873d0196-7d12-54d8-873f-8fcafdad832e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "873d0196-7d12-54d8-873f-8fcafdad832e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354330} :answer-field/id #uuid "1167940f-f8af-53d3-9608-53c4a11b55dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9a9fcbb-0956-5f3b-af49-06a82980ec40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9a9fcbb-0956-5f3b-af49-06a82980ec40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354332} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fba3f53-6ecb-5f02-9210-5fbcb22b241c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88808e5e-044c-5693-8818-e1b8d2c67ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88808e5e-044c-5693-8818-e1b8d2c67ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354334} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eea284c-78e7-51dd-bdb6-1a3e49da4a80" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0de8cf89-d134-59d7-b539-d68786c14110" :question/math-academy-id "q-351326" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{-{x}^{2}+7x+1}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{-{x}^{2}+7x+1}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $13({x}^{2}+9)$ |  | $13(x-2)$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$, and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+7x+1}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{C}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)$ gives\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+7x+1=(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+9).\n$$\n\nFor $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(2)^{2}+7(2)+1 & =C(4+9) \\\\ 11 & =13C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(0)^{2}+7(0)+1 & =(A(0)+B)(0-2)+\\frac{11}{13}((0)^{2}+9) \\\\ 1 & =-2B+\\frac{99}{13} \\\\ -2B & =-\\frac{86}{13} \\\\ B & =\\frac{43}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(1)^{2}+7(1)+1 & =(A(1)+\\frac{43}{13})(1-2)+\\frac{11}{13}((1)^{2}+9) \\\\ 7 & =-A-\\frac{43}{13}+\\frac{110}{13} \\\\ -A & =\\frac{24}{13} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{24}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+7x+1}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{24}{13}x+\\frac{43}{13})}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{(\\frac{11}{13})}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24\\,x+43}{13({x}^{2}+9)}+\\frac{11}{13(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cee2fd-a19b-5282-928d-d6049e8e5652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266510} {:answer/id #uuid "db591d1e-7e6d-5d66-b8f4-84876d3f3b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266511} {:answer/id #uuid "c094c537-84f6-517f-b8b2-741924c48efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266512} {:answer/id #uuid "b1fb1492-0e56-58be-bc9b-846911d9b217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266513} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb8c9c6-97c9-5ce1-9cf6-e327c0dec48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db591d1e-7e6d-5d66-b8f4-84876d3f3b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266511} :answer-field/id #uuid "973d406d-b59e-51f8-9592-60618ef9c468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14c8c7b9-ef62-5de2-a5cf-13ef9a69fd54" :question/math-academy-id "q-51148" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+4}{({x}^{2}+4)x}=\\frac{-x+B}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+4)x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =(-x+B)x+({x}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)+4 & =(-1+B)(1)+({1}^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =-1+B+5 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
